A <generalized linear model> has three components:

* a random component in which independent responses belong to an exponential dispersion family;
* a systematic component $\eta_i=x_i^T\beta$;
* a link <function> satisfying $g(\mu_i)=\eta_i$, where $\mu_i=\mathbb E Y_i$.

Solved by gpt-5.6-sol high.

= b
{parent=5l}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Write the exponential-dispersion density as
$$
f(y_i;\theta_i,\phi)
=\exp\left\{\frac{y_i\theta_i-b(\theta_i)}{\phi}+c(y_i,\phi)\right\}.
$$
With the canonical link, $\theta_i=\eta_i=x_i^T\beta$. Therefore
$$
\boxed{
\mathbb E Y_i=b'(x_i^T\beta),
\qquad
\operatorname{Var}(Y_i)=\phi b''(x_i^T\beta)}.
$$
Known prior weights replace $\phi$ by $\phi/w_i$.

Solved by gpt-5.6-sol high.

= c
{parent=5l}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

In the Poisson GLM the responses are independent and
$$
Y_i\sim\operatorname{Poisson}(\mu_i).
$$
The <Poisson canonical link> is logarithmic, so
$$
\boxed{\log\mu_i=x_i^T\beta,
\qquad \mu_i=e^{x_i^T\beta},
\qquad \operatorname{Var}(Y_i)=\mu_i}.
$$

Solved by gpt-5.6-sol high.

